\section{Methodology}

We perform an idealized, collisionless $N$-body experiment designed to isolate
the emergence of macroscopic irreversibility and stellar disk heating during a
major galaxy merger. The simulation is intentionally constructed to preserve
formal time reversibility at the algorithmic level, such that any observed
irreversibility can be attributed to phase-space coarse-graining, numerical
discretization, and dynamical mixing rather than explicit dissipation.

\subsection{Physical Setup}

The system models a simplified Milky Way--Andromeda (MW--M31) merger composed of
four collisionless components: stellar disks and dark matter halos for each
galaxy. Each component is represented by an ensemble of equal-mass particles.
No gas dynamics, star formation, or feedback processes are included.

Dark matter halos follow a Hernquist density profile with scale length $a$ and
total mass $M$. Particle positions are sampled directly from the analytic
cumulative distribution function. Halo velocities are initialized to zero,
placing the halos in a deliberately cold, non-equilibrium configuration. This
choice maximizes subsequent phase mixing and enhances sensitivity to
irreversibility diagnostics.

Stellar disks follow an exponential surface density profile with scale length
$R_d$ and a Gaussian vertical distribution with scale height $h_z$. Disk
particles are assigned azimuthal velocities corresponding to approximate
centrifugal support,
\begin{equation}
v_\phi(R) = \sqrt{\frac{G M}{R + \epsilon}},
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon$ is a small regularization term. The disks are not constructed
to be in detailed equilibrium; secular heating and diffusion arising from this
imbalance are treated as part of the experiment rather than as numerical error.

The two galaxies are initially separated by $780\,\mathrm{kpc}$ along the
$x$-axis. Andromeda is given an initial bulk velocity of
$-120\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$ along the $x$ direction, producing an idealized
head-on encounter.

\subsection{Gravitational Dynamics}

Gravitational forces are computed using direct summation of Newtonian gravity
with Plummer softening,
\begin{equation}
\Phi(r) = -\frac{G m}{\sqrt{r^2 + \epsilon^2}},
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon$ is the softening length. The force solver scales as
$\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ and is intentionally naive to ensure transparency and strict
determinism.

Time integration is performed using a kick--drift--kick leapfrog scheme, which
is symplectic and formally time reversible. This choice ensures that any
macroscopic arrow of time observed in the simulation does not originate from
the integrator itself.

\subsection{Numerical Configuration}

The system is evolved for $120$ timesteps with a fixed timestep of
$50\,\mathrm{Myr}$. All particles have equal mass within each component.
Snapshots are recorded at every timestep for post-processing analysis.

Global conserved quantities, including total energy and angular momentum, are
tracked for numerical validation. While small violations arise from force
softening and floating-point truncation, these effects remain subdominant
compared to the irreversible trends revealed by coarse-grained diagnostics.

\subsection{Phase-Space Irreversibility}

Irreversibility is quantified using coarse-grained phase-space measures.
At each snapshot, the six-dimensional phase-space distribution
$(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{v})$ is discretized into a fixed Cartesian grid with
$N_\mathrm{bin}^6$ cells. The resulting histogram provides an estimate of the
coarse-grained phase-space density $f(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{v})$.

To measure divergence between distributions at different times, we compute the
Kullback--Leibler (KL) divergence,
\begin{equation}
D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = \sum_i P_i \ln \frac{P_i}{Q_i},
\end{equation}
where $P$ and $Q$ are normalized phase-space density estimates. Growth of
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ under formally reversible dynamics is interpreted as evidence
of emergent macroscopic irreversibility driven by phase mixing and coarse
graining.

\subsection{Disk Heating Diagnostics}

Stellar disk heating is quantified using kinematic measures. In particular, we
compute the vertical velocity dispersion,
\begin{equation}
\sigma_z^2 = \mathrm{Var}(v_z),
\end{equation}
as a function of time. Increases in $\sigma_z$ correspond to disk thickening and
loss of ordered motion.

We also track the $z$-component of specific angular momentum,
\begin{equation}
L_z = x v_y - y v_x,
\end{equation}
to characterize angular momentum redistribution during the merger.

\subsection{Design Philosophy}

The code prioritizes physical interpretability and methodological clarity over
computational efficiency. All assumptions are implemented explicitly rather
than implicitly. The simulation is intended not as a realistic reproduction of
the Local Group merger, but as a controlled dynamical laboratory for studying
the emergence of irreversibility in collisionless self-gravitating systems.

All scientific analysis is performed in post-processing to maintain a strict
separation between dynamics and diagnostics.
